// Two sibling modules bind the alias `Shared` to two different files. The
// compiler must reject this rather than silently pick one.
//
// `all_aliases` in main.sf is a single table for the whole compilation, not one
// per file, and the write was guarded by `if (!all_aliases.has(ma))`. First
// module walked won; every later binding was dropped without a word. The
// program compiled, linked, exited 0 — and called the wrong module's function.
// A wrong answer with a green exit code is worse than a crash, because nothing
// prompts you to look.
//
// This was not hypothetical: src/lib/ast.sf, src/lib/lexer.sf and
// src/lib/parser.sf each bound `Internal` to a different compiler file, so
// `Internal.Parser(...)` in parser.sf resolved against compiler/ast.sf and died
// at link time with an undefined symbol.
//
// Per-file alias tables are the real fix — `all_aliases` is threaded all the
// way into codegen's `module_prefixes` — so for now the compiler refuses
// instead of guessing. Rebinding the same alias to the *same* file stays legal;
// many files import "@os" as OS. Only a genuine disagreement is an error.
import "../fixtures/alias_collision_use_alpha.sf" as UseAlpha
import "../fixtures/alias_collision_use_beta.sf" as UseBeta

// If this ever compiles again, both lines print "alpha".
IO.println(UseAlpha.ask())
IO.println(UseBeta.ask())
